R["«O71»"] = """**(c)** Training on the legacy-logged data gives almost the same ranking quality: **AUC 0.7870** (CI 0.7773 to 0.7987) against **0.7925** (0.7810 to 0.8034) for the randomised control set, so the CIs overlap and we cannot separate them. The differences are elsewhere. The legacy model's training labels show an RTO rate of **27.1% against 31.7%** (z = -12.2, p = 1.9e-34), because **16.8%** of legacy orders were blocked and **56.2%** of those would have been RTO had they shipped. Its calibration is off (PCOC **0.971** vs **1.004**). Its recall at the cap is higher (**29.6%** vs **23.7%**), but its realised false-positive rate is **4.65%** against the 3% cap, while the control model realises **2.78%**; the extra recall is bought with extra false positives, because the cap was set on a training set missing the riskiest orders. The time trend is not significant: odds ratio **1.012 per month** (95% CI 1.000 to 1.024, p = 0.058). **(f)** Selection bias here distorts calibration and the false-positive cap more than AUC. The randomised holdout is what makes the cap reliable, and the data are stable enough over the window that a first-150-days / last-30-days split is fair."""
R["«O72»"] = """**(c)** Match precision rises as the threshold tightens: **94.3%** at 0.4, **96.6%** at 0.5, **98.1%** at 0.6, **99.7%** at 0.8, and 100% at 0.9 and above, while the share of found profiles kept falls from **93.7%** to **86.2%** (at 0.6), **54.9%** (at 0.8) and **7.5%** (at 0.95). The chi-square on right/wrong counts at 0.4, 0.6 and 0.8 rejects equal precision (chi2 = 223.3, p = 3.3e-49). The model effect is small: test AUC is **0.8053** at 0.4, **0.8066** at 0.6 and **0.7920** at 0.95, and the CIs overlap heavily across 0.4 to 0.8. **(f)** A threshold near **0.6** keeps most of the coverage (43% of users matched) with 98% correct matches, and AUC is flat in that region, so there is no accuracy reason to accept the 6% wrong matches at 0.4. Only the very strict thresholds (0.9 and above) give up coverage and some AUC."""
R["«O73»"] = """**(c)** Gradient boosting has the highest AUC family (**0.8066** with all features), Random Forest is close (**0.7987** with quantitative social features, 0.7985 with the embedding) and logistic regression is lowest (**0.7580** history only, 0.7805 and 0.7804 with social features). The CIs of the tree models overlap, so Random Forest and gradient boosting are not clearly different; logistic regression with history only is clearly below both. Social features raise AUC in every family: **+0.0225** for logistic regression (history to quantitative), **+0.0121** for Random Forest and **+0.0127** for gradient boosting. The embedding adds nothing for logistic regression and Random Forest, and **+0.0014** for gradient boosting. Recall at the cap ranges from **19.8%** to **25.9%** and the realised false-positive rate stays between **2.8% and 3.3%**, so the cap is held on the unseen month. **(f)** Non-linear trees on history features are worth about **0.03 AUC** over logistic regression. The social gain is smaller than the model-family gain on all orders, but it adds on top of it."""
R["«O74»"] = """**(c)** For gradient boosting on all test orders, adding the quantitative social features raises AUC by **0.0127** (CI 0.0085 to 0.0170) and recall by **1.4 points** (CI 0.3 to 2.5), with 100 RTO orders caught only by the new model and 70 only by the old (McNemar p = 0.026, Holm p = 0.052). The embedding step alone is borderline: ΔAUC **0.0014** (CI 0.0001 to 0.0027), p_holm = 0.060. From history only to the full model, the gain is **ΔAUC 0.0141**, **Δrecall +2.2 points** (0.96 to 3.30; 110 vs 63 orders; p_holm = **0.0013**) and **+56.6 bps** of Goodness (CI 23.5 to 86.8). For **re-identified users** the gain is larger: **ΔAUC 0.0325** (CI 0.0234 to 0.0420), **Δrecall +5.7 points** (CI 3.2 to 8.1), 97 vs 42 orders, p_holm < 1e-4 and **+148.9 bps** Goodness. For **thin-history users** (at most one past order): **ΔAUC 0.0208**, **Δrecall +4.4 points** and +107.4 bps, p_holm < 1e-4; here the embedding step is also significant by recall (+1.9 points, p_holm = 0.0079) though its ΔAUC CI includes zero. **(f)** Social data is clearly useful, but the benefit sits with re-identified and thin-history users; for all orders the gain is about 1.4 points of AUC and 2 points of recall. The embedding adds little beyond the quantitative profile features and should not be used on its own as the reason to collect text data."""
R["«O75»"] = """at the 3% cap the final model (gradient boosting, all features; threshold 0.674) flags **10.2%** of orders with **precision 81.6%** (CI 79.0% to 84.3%), **recall 25.9%** (CI 24.3% to 27.9%), a false-positive rate of **2.78%** (CI 2.36% to 3.25%) and Goodness of **562 bps** (CI 511 to 614). Cap sensitivity: at 1% the recall is **13.7%** (precision 87.9%), at 5% it is **33.4%** (77.4%) and at 10% it is **46.0%** (69.0%), so recall rises quickly as customer friction rises. Calibration is not perfect: the slope is **1.106** (CI 1.046 to 1.165; the model is slightly under-confident, so true risk varies a bit more than predicted), intercept +0.051, PCOC **1.005**, and Hosmer-Lemeshow **rejects** (chi2 = 22.9, df 8, p = 0.0035). With 6,745 test orders the test can detect small misfit, but the cost rule in 7.7 relies on these probabilities, so recalibration (for example isotonic) should be applied before using it. The recall of 25.9% is below the paper's reported 40% to 60%. We did not tune the simulation to match it."""
R["«O76»"] = """the omnibus test across occupation types rejects equal false-positive rates (**chi2 = 11.5, p = 0.042**, 5 df), but weakly: **senior managers** are wrongly flagged least (**1.44%**, CI 0.66% to 3.10%) and **other / not stated** most (**4.15%**), then self-employed (3.50%), students (3.46%), early career (2.39%) and mid-career professionals (2.12%). No pairwise comparison with the lowest group survives Holm correction (smallest p_holm **0.067**, other / not stated), and the CIs overlap widely, so the evidence is suggestive rather than conclusive (the senior-manager group has only 418 good orders). Flag rates follow each group's RTO rate (for example 14.5% for students and for other / not stated, which have RTO rates of 36.2% and 40.9%, against 6.4% for mid-career professionals at 23.6%). The social features change the gap only for senior managers (FPR 2.63% history only, 1.44% with all features). Users with a re-identified profile have a flag rate of **11.3%** against **9.4%**, a lower false-positive rate (**2.58%** vs 2.92%) and higher recall (**28.5%** vs 23.8%). The model never sees occupation type, so any difference here comes through proxy features and history. Audit these gaps on real data before launch."""
R["«O77»"] = """in the base case (RTO cost ₹180, nudge success ρ = 50%, loss φ = 25% of flagged good orders, 12% margin) the **capped threshold saves ₹6,768 per 1,000 orders** (CI 6,146 to 7,426) and the **cost-optimal rule ₹17,689** (CI 16,629 to 18,761), a difference of **₹10,921** per 1,000 orders (CI 10,024 to 11,854; p = 3.8e-106). The cost rule gets there by flagging **62.8%** of orders, with recall **85%** but precision only **43.5%** and a false-positive rate of **52.3%**; that is nudging more than half of all good COD customers, which the cost formula treats as a 25% loss each time and does not capture beyond that (repeat purchases, brand damage). The capped saving stays positive across the whole grid, from **₹3,331** (ρ 30%, φ 40%) to **₹10,206** (ρ 70%, φ 10%). **A/B size:** the capped model flags about 10.2% of orders with 81.6% RTO among them, so to detect a nudge that cuts RTO among flagged orders by **20%** we need 112 flagged orders per arm and **2,193 COD orders** in total, by **35%** 39 per arm (**764** orders) and by **50%** 19 per arm (**373** orders). These numbers cover only the RTO outcome; measuring the loss of good orders (φ) as a second outcome needs a larger sample, because it is a rate among about 90% of the orders."""
R["«O9»"] = """at 1 lakh COD orders a day, the **capped rule is worth about ₹24.7 crore a year** in the base case (CI 22.6 to 27.0), **₹12.2 crore** in the worst case (ρ 30%, φ 40%; CI 10.5 to 13.8) and **₹37.3 crore** in the best case (ρ 70%, φ 10%; CI 34.4 to 40.2), flagging 10.2% of orders. The cost-optimal rule would show **₹22.8 crore** in the worst case, **₹64.6 crore** in the base case (CI 61.3 to 68.5) and **₹124.5 crore** in the best case, but it flags **36.4%, 62.8% and 91.0%** of orders respectively, so it is no longer a targeted risk model and ignores customer-experience cost. The CIs only reflect sampling of test orders. The assumed nudge success ρ and good-order loss φ move the answer by about a factor of three, and the A/B test should measure them."""
R["«O10»"] = """**Recommendation**

1. **Deploy the history-based gradient boosting model with a 3% false-positive cap** (threshold 0.674 here; set it on out-of-fold scores) as the first COD risk flag. Expect about 82% of flagged orders to be real RTO orders and about a quarter of all RTO orders to be caught.
2. **Add social-profile features only for users matched at a score of 0.6 or higher** (98% match precision), and only after legal and privacy review. The gain is about +0.014 AUC on all orders and +0.033 for re-identified users.
3. **Keep a 5% randomised holdout without intervention** and train on it, so labels, calibration and the cap stay unbiased.
4. **Recalibrate before using the probabilities for a cost rule** (slope 1.106, Hosmer-Lemeshow p = 0.0035), and do not move to the cost-optimal rule (62.8% of orders flagged) until the A/B test measures how many good customers are lost.
5. **Run the nudge A/B test**: about 764 COD orders in total to detect a 35% cut in RTO among flagged orders, with the good-order loss as a second outcome.

**Decision rule:** roll out if RTO among nudged flagged orders falls by at least 20% against the control and the loss of good-order revenue stays at or below the assumed 25%; stop if the false-positive rate on the holdout exceeds the 3% cap by more than 1 point.

**Not yet known:** the real nudge effect and good-order loss, whether false-positive gaps across customer groups exist on real data, the recall of our model on real orders (25.9% here against 40% to 60% in the paper), and the legal basis for profile matching."""
